\\"]\\n21:T151e,\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"Shri Navkhanda Parshwanath. 108 Parshwanath, No. 55 – Shri Navkhanda Parshwanath. Main Deity: A black image of Lord Shri Navkhanda Parshwanath in the padmasana posture, 36 inches tall and 24.25 inches wide. Above the head of the image is a canopy of nine serpent hoods.. Location: This holy site is at the port of Ghogha.. History: The ancient name of Ghogha was Gundigadh. It was a very famous port. In V.S. 1138, on the teaching of the revered Acharya Shri Mahendrasuriji Maharajsaheb, Seth Shri Hirubhai Nanavati of the Shrimali community had a magnificent jinalaya of Lord Parshwanath built. This temple therefore dates from before the 12th century. For years this shrine offered noble souls an unparalleled inspiration on the path to liberation. During Muslim rule, when other temples and images were destroyed, this temple too was attacked. The image was broken into nine pieces, put into a sack, and the sack thrown into a well in Bhavnagar, where it remained for many years. One day, guided by a divine sign, a shravaka pulled this image out of the well and brought the broken pieces to Ghogha. A divine power instructed that the image be kept for 9 days in \\\"9 mann of lapsi\\\" (a preparation of cracked wheat, ghee, jaggery, and water). Everyone eagerly awaited the completion of all 9 days, but on the 8th day a sangh (pilgrim group) arrived from Bharuch, eager for darshan of the Lord, and so the image was taken out on the 8th day itself. It had joined together completely, but the marks where the nine pieces met can still be seen on the image even today. For this reason this form of Lord Parshwanath came to be known as Navkhanda Parshwanath (\\\"Lord of Nine Parts\\\"), since the image has 9 joins. A saffron-coloured flame burns continuously here from a lamp. It is believed that when something of ours is lost, chanting the name of \\\"Navkhand Parshwanath\\\" 108 times will help recover the lost object.. Other Temples: There are 4 other temples near this temple. Two other large temples in the village belong to Chandraprabhuji and Jiravala Parshwanath.. Art and Sculpture: The craftsmanship of this image is exquisite, ancient, radiant, and miraculous. At the nearby temple of Lord Shri Neminath, many images were recovered from beneath the ground — ancient, artistic images made of the five metals (panchdhatu). In the nearby \\\"Samosaran\\\" temple there is a metal samavasarana dating to the 16th century. Adjoining the main temple are the temples of Lord Suvidhinath and Lord Shantinath. It is believed that the architectural style of the village's temple of Lord Chandraprabhu dates from the era of King Kumarpal. Ghogha also has the remains of several ancient Jain temples. On Saturday, the 3rd day of the bright half of Falgun, Vikram Samvat 2075, this temple was renovated and reconsecrated with the Anjanshalaka ceremony.. Directions: The nearest railway station is Bhavnagar, 21 km away, connected to major cities across India. Bhavnagar airport is 20 km away, with a daily flight from Mumbai. Palitana is 56 km from here. Buses and taxis are available, and there is a paved road to the Jain temple. Dharamshala and bhojanshala facilities are available.. Scripture References: This temple is mentioned in \\\"Tirthmala,\\\" \\\"Chaityaparipati,\\\" \\\"Parshwanath Nammala,\\\" and others. Images of Navkhanda Parshwanath are also found at the Panchasar Parshwanath temple in Patan, the Shantinath temple at Shankhalpur, the Kalikund Parshwanath temple, and at Bhildiyaji Tirtha.. Hymn of Praise: In battles with foreign invaders you were broken into nine parts, yet you remain, O Lord, unbroken bliss for me; O soul, seek out the one who finds what is lost. \\\"With loving devotion I bow in reverence to Shri Navkhanda Parshwa.\\\". Chanting Mantra: \\\"Om Hreem Arham Shri Navkhanda Parshwanathaya Namah\\\". Trust: Sheth Kala Mitha ni Pedhi, Navkhanda Parshwanath Jain Derasar, Ghogha: 364110, District: Bhavnagar, State of Gujarat, India. Phone – 0278-2282335.. The Life of the Reverend (Purushdani) Arhat Parshwanath, Part 7 (The Birth Celebration and Naming of Lord Parshwa): When, early in the morning, the maidservant brought King Ashvasen the joyful news of the birth of his son, the King, overjoyed, rewarded her with wealth, jewels, and precious stones, relieving her of poverty. King Ashvasen freed the prisoners held in the jail and celebrated the birth festival of Lord Parshwa. On the first day, in accordance with family tradition, the customary rites were duly performed; on the third day, the ceremony of showing the child the sun and moon was completed. On the night of the sixth day, in keeping with family custom, the parents held a night vigil (ratri jagaran mahotsav). In this way, performing all the prescribed rites, eleven days passed, and rites such as the cutting of the umbilical cord were also completed. On the twelfth day, King Ashvasen had a great feast of delicacies, dried fruits, and mukhwas (mouth fresheners) prepared in the morning, and invited his friends, relatives, and servants to the meal. Mother Vama had, while the Lord was still in the womb, seen a serpent passing by her on a dark-fortnight night, and had told King Ashvasen of this. Recognising this as a sign of the child's influence while in the womb, King Ashvasen, after the feast was complete, named his son Parshwa before all present. Gradually, under proper care and much affection, the child Parshwa grew up.\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1b:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mb-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"a\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"https://goo.gl/maps/AMBg3VWw68ggt52y6\\\",\\\"target\\\":\\\"_blank\\\",\\\"rel\\\":\\\"noopener noreferrer\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-sm bg-maroon px-5 py-2.5 font-ui text-sm font-medium text-ivory hover:bg-maroon-deep transition-colors\\\",\