paralleled inspiration on the path to liberation. During Muslim rule, when other temples and images were destroyed, this temple too was attacked. The image was broken into nine pieces, put into a sack, and the sack thrown into a well in Bhavnagar, where it remained for many years. One day, guided by a divine sign, a shravaka pulled this image out of the well and brought the broken pieces to Ghogha. A divine power instructed that the image be kept for 9 days in \\\"9 mann of lapsi\\\" (a preparation of cracked wheat, ghee, jaggery, and water). Everyone eagerly awaited the completion of all 9 days, but on the 8th day a sangh (pilgrim group) arrived from Bharuch, eager for darshan of the Lord, and so the image was taken out on the 8th day itself. It had joined together completely, but the marks where the nine pieces met can still be seen on the image even today. For this reason this form of Lord Parshwanath came to be known as Navkhanda Parshwanath (\\\"Lord of Nine Parts\\\"), since the image has 9 joins. A saffron-coloured flame burns continuously here from a lamp. It is believed that when something of ours is lost, chanting the name of \\\"Navkhand Parshwanath\\\" 108 times will help recover the lost object.\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1d:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"article\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"0\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"108 Parshwanath, No. 55 – Shri Navkhanda Parshwanath\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"1\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Main Deity: A black image of Lord Shri Navkhanda Parshwanath in the padmasana posture, 36 inches tall and 24.25 inches wide. Above the head of the image is a canopy of nine serpent hoods.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"2\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Location: This holy site is at the port of Ghogha.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"3\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"$23\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"4\\\",{\\\"children\\